+## 1. Decision: Orca coordinates, commands stay the units of work
+
+### 1.1 The decision
+An Orca coordinator plans the work, creates Tasks, starts one worker per Task and reads each worker's report. Each IronClaude `/sc:` command stays a self-contained unit of work: one worker (a top-level Claude session in its own Orca terminal) runs one command and reports it with `worker_done`. Commands themselves contain no Orca calls. What changes in the commands is limited to the worker contract in section 2: a way to run without a person, a fixed report file, and a resume or safe re-run.
+
+### 1.2 Evidence for the decision
+| # | Claim | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| E1 | Orca's model is a coordinator that starts workers; a Run does not schedule work itself, so something above the commands must do the planning. | `[G]` lines 51-52, quoted at `CAPS:92`; ready-wave pattern at `CAPS:86` |
+| E2 | A worker is already a top-level Claude session, so a command run by a worker has the same tools as a command a person runs. | `CAPS:161` (`[H:worker-start]` line 38, `--agent claude`); `.dev/evals/ccsession-orca-e2e-20261026/EVIDENCE.md:45` (worker reached `state: ready, stage: input_accepted` and answered) |
+| E3 | It has worked before: in the codegraph planning run, Orca workers invoked `/sc:adversarial`, and that work landed as PR #329. | `.dev/codegraph-migration/orchestration/README.md:31,33`; `.dev/orca-command-refactor/README.md:12`; return contract `invocation_method: "skill-direct"` at `.dev/codegraph-migration/decisions/P3-command-set/adversarial/return-contract.yaml:10` |
+| E4 | Commands keep their own internal fan-out. Agent-tool subagents inside a worker are not Orca workers and are not counted by Orca's nesting limit. | `CAPS:155` (`nested-worker-depth.js:7-11,18-22`); fan-out per command in column (c) of `INV-A:35-55` and `INV-B:31-52` |
+| E5 | Nothing has to be removed: no command, skill, agent or core file calls Orca today. The only Orca-aware primitive is the `ccsession-tag` launcher. | `grep -rlni orca src/superclaude/{commands,skills,core,agents}` (run 2026-11-02) lists only files under `sk/ccsession-tag/`; `grep -ci orca src/superclaude/core/HOW-WE-WORK.md` printed `0` |
+| E6 | The gaps the inventories found are person-blocking gates, missing report files, interface drift and shared-state clobbering. None is fixed by making commands call Orca, and all must be fixed under either design. | `INV-A:76-83`, `INV-A:94-98`; `INV-B:289-299` |
+
+### 1.3 Rejected alternative: commands call Orca internally
+In the alternative, a command would itself start Orca workers for its fan-out (for example one worker per `/sc:tdd` research agent), send its own `worker_done`, and call `orca orchestration ask` for its questions. It is rejected for these reasons:
+
+| # | Reason | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| R1 | It cannot run as a worker at the current setting. Nesting depth is 1 on this machine and "1 means a coordinator dispatches workers and those workers may not dispatch further", so a command that calls `worker-start` from inside a worker would be refused with `nested_worker_depth_exceeded`. Which call sites enforce the check was not traced; `worker-start` and `dispatch` are presumed (UNVERIFIED, `CAPS:103`). A new Run does not reset depth, and Orca's own advice is "Do the work in this terminal". The two designs exclude each other unless the user raises the depth setting. | `CAPS:97-101,103`; `orca-data.json:1` quoted at `CAPS:98` |
+| R2 | It breaks standalone use. Commands are installed to `~/.claude/` and run in other projects. Without a running Orca every orchestration call fails with `runtime_unavailable`; outside an Orca terminal the caller must pass `--from`; and a session with no live preamble must not send lifecycle messages at all. | `CLAUDE.md:74`; `CAPS:147` (`cli-error.js:49-53`); `CAPS:148-149` (`[G]` line 41; `terminal-identity.js:213-215`) |
+| R3 | A command cannot own the completion message. `worker_done` is accepted only from the dispatched pane, and the Task and Dispatch IDs exist only in the preamble given to the worker session, not in the command's arguments. | `CAPS:76`, `CAPS:150` (`[H:send]` line 40); `CAPS:148` (`[G]` line 38) |
+| R4 | The executable name differs by platform and setup (`orca-ide` on Linux outside Orca terminals, an `orca` shim inside them), so command text that calls Orca would carry platform logic. | `CAPS:145` (`/opt/Orca/resources/bin/orca-ide:16`; `linux-orca-cli-shim/orca:2`) |
+| R5 | It would create a second orchestrator below the coordinator. The inventories already mark the commands that orchestrate (`/sc:agent`, `/sc:pm`, `/sc:spawn`) coordinator-only because two controllers compete. | `INV-A:90`; `INV-B:285-286` |
+
+What the alternative would have given, and how this spec gets it instead: per-unit liveness, Task records and independent retry for heavy fan-out. Orca keeps those records only for workers the coordinator starts (`CAPS:158`). Under this decision the coordinator gets them by splitting the work into separate worker Tasks (for example several `/sc:troubleshoot` workers, then one `/sc:adversarial` worker on their reports), not by commands calling Orca.
+
+### 1.4 How strong the evidence is
+The evidence supports the decision. Three gaps are stated plainly:
+1. **No command in scope has been run end to end as an Orca worker in this run.** Every verdict in both inventories comes from reading files (`INV-B:323`). The only end-to-end proof is the PR #329 run, and its brief told workers to run `/sc:adversarial` through an Agent-tool subagent (`.dev/codegraph-migration/orchestration/README.md:31`), so it proves the nested route, not the direct route this spec requires. Whether every such spawn succeeded is UNVERIFIED (`CAPS:156`, `CAPS:187`). Phase 0 (section 6) therefore starts with live smoke runs.
+2. **The 60-second readiness evidence is narrower than it looks.** It concerns a question shown *before* Claude reached its input box (`EVIDENCE.md:39`, a shell wrapper's session-name prompt). A command's own `AskUserQuestion` or chat question comes *after* readiness, so it does not fail `worker-start`; Orca reports such a worker as waiting, "healthy, not failed" (`CAPS:52`, `CAPS:129`). The real failure mode for a command prompt is a silent stall with no `worker_done`, which the coordinator sees only through `worker-show`'s `agentWait` or a missing report. Section 2 covers both cases.
+3. **Agent-tool fan-out inside a worker has no proven limit, but no proof of none either.** No Orca limit on subagents was found, and whether a subagent's Bash `send` would be accepted as the worker's own is UNVERIFIED (`CAPS:155`, `CAPS:159-160`, `CAPS:186`). The contract forbids subagents from sending lifecycle messages, so the design does not depend on that answer.
+
+## 2. The worker contract
+
+### 2.1 Terms
+- **Worker run**: a session that received an Orca preamble carrying a Task ID and a Dispatch ID. Only such a session may send lifecycle messages; a session the user started, inside or outside Orca, is an ordinary session (`CAPS:148`, `[G]` lines 38 and 41).
+- **Unattended mode**: the behaviour a command switches to when its caller passes `--unattended`. It is the only command-side change the contract asks for besides report files and resume. It names no Orca command, so the same mode serves any caller that has no person at the keyboard. How the mode is selected (flag versus detecting the preamble) is open decision D1 in section 9; this spec is written for the recommended flag.
+- **Contract file**: one shared document, proposed as `src/superclaude/core/WORKER-CONTRACT.md`, installed to `~/.claude/WORKER-CONTRACT.md`. Install copies every `*.md` in `src/superclaude/core/` to `~/.claude/` (`src/superclaude/cli/install_core.py:4,33,53-70`; `CLAUDE.md:83,408`), so the new file installs with no code change. `KNOWN_CORE_FILES` (`src/superclaude/cli/install_core.py:12-24`) only filters the installed-files listing (`install_core.py:152`); the new name is added there so the listing reports it. Each changed command adds a short `## Unattended mode` section whose first line is "Read `~/.claude/WORKER-CONTRACT.md` before any other step", because an installed core file that `CLAUDE.md` does not import is read only when something tells the agent to read it (the routing guide is documented as "not loaded automatically", `src/superclaude/core/CLAUDE.md:46,75`); the section then lists only the command's own gate resolutions and report file, so the shared rules are written once and still applied.
+- **Worker contract**: the WC-4 file `worker-contract.yaml`, written at the path the caller passes with `--contract <path>`. It is a separate file from any `return-contract.yaml` a command already writes, so existing contracts keep their own schema and status values unchanged.
+
+### 2.2 Requirements
+Each requirement says what the command must do, what the worker session (the top-level agent that received the preamble) must do, and the evidence.
+
+| ID | Requirement | Owner | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| WC-1 | **No prompt before the agent is ready.** Nothing in the launch path (wrapper, hook, startup command) may wait on a person before Claude's input box is ready. Any such prompt must fall back on its own well inside the readiness limit (default 60 s). | Launch setup, not commands | `EVIDENCE.md:39` (worker-start failed at `agent_readiness` behind a session-name prompt); `EVIDENCE.md:45,56` (passes with a 30 s fallback; above about 45 s it fails again); `CAPS:44-45` (60 s default, `--timeout-ms` per start) |
+| WC-2 | **No person-blocking step in unattended mode.** Under `--unattended` the command never calls `AskUserQuestion`, never ends its turn on a chat question or `[yes / no]` offer, and never says STOP AND WAIT. Each gate resolves one of three ways, listed per command in section 3: (a) a documented default; (b) a value the caller supplied as a flag; (c) **blocked**: the command writes its report with `status: blocked` and the exact question, and stops. | Command | Gates per command at `INV-A:76-83`, `INV-B:292`; a worker waiting on a prompt is "healthy", so a stall is invisible (`CAPS:52`, `CAPS:129`); a plain chat offer also stalls (`INV-A:62`) |
+| WC-3 | **Questions go to the coordinator, not to a local prompt.** When a command ends `blocked` and the worker session holds a preamble, the session may ask the coordinator with the preamble's `ask` command, wait, then re-invoke the command with the answer. Rules: (1) A gate is answerable through `ask` only when its section 3 row names the flag or argument that carries the answer; any other gate (for example cli-portify's approval gates, implement's Ruling) is not answerable this way, and the worker reports the blocked contract. (2) At most one `ask` per gate per Dispatch; a second gate hit after the re-run gets its own single `ask`. (3) The re-run uses the same command line plus the answering flag, the same `--contract` path and the same output location; it overwrites the blocked worker contract, which is the only file a re-run is meant to replace (WC-6 freshness applies per Dispatch, not per re-run). (4) An empty reply, or a reply that names no usable value, counts as no answer: the worker keeps the blocked contract and reports it. (5) On an `ask` timeout the session resumes the same message ID and never creates a duplicate, at most 3 times (4 waits in all); after that it reports the blocked contract. No heartbeat is sent while blocked in `ask`. | Worker session | `CAPS:58-61` (`[H:ask]` lines 3-5, 23-24; `[G]` lines 80-82); `CAPS:64` (`[G:worker-contract]` lines 10-11) |
+| WC-4 | **One worker contract, at a path known before the command runs, written on every outcome.** `--unattended` requires `--contract <path>`; the command refuses `--unattended` without it (a chat-only refusal, since there is no path to write). The command writes `worker-contract.yaml` at that path on success, failure, blocked and partial outcomes, including stops before any other output exists (for workflow, pre-package STOPs such as `E-LEGACY`, which today write no yaml by design). One flag serves every output class: directory outputs (`--output <dir>`), file outputs (tdd and init-lite take a file, `cmd/tdd.md:49`, `cmd/init-lite.md:32`), generated packages (workflow's `<id>` is generated and `--output` is retired) and nested paths (adversarial's own contract). The file is separate from any `return-contract.yaml` a command already writes; those keep their schema and values unchanged (SA-1). Fields, at least: `contract_version` (`"1.0"`), `status` (one of `succeeded`, `partial`, `failed`, `blocked`), `verdict` (`PASS`, `FAIL` or `n/a`; `PASS`/`FAIL` for audit, review and validation commands, `n/a` otherwise), `command` (the exact command line), `report_path` (the main human-readable deliverable, or null), `artifacts` (every other file written, including the command's own `return-contract.yaml` if any), `blocked_question` (when blocked), and `next_command` (when `blocked` or `failed`: the exact command a person or the coordinator runs to finish or resume, or null when none exists). Translation from a command's own status, where it has one: `success` → `succeeded`; `partial` → `partial`; `failed` → `failed`; brainstorm `dry-run` (only when the brief passed `--dry-run`) → `succeeded`; troubleshoot `blocked` (a hard stop that needs an external action, report still written) → `blocked`, with that external action as `blocked_question`. The worker passes the `--contract` path as `worker_done --report-path`. Orca does not check the file, so the coordinator opens it. | Command writes; worker reports; coordinator reads | `CAPS:72-74` (`--report-path` only with real values); `CAPS:79` (Orca's handling of the file is UNVERIFIED); existing status values: `sk/sc-workflow-protocol/refs/return-contract.md:6-7,39`, `sk/sc-brainstorm-protocol/SKILL.md:340-341`, `sk/sc-troubleshoot-protocol/SKILL.md:65` |
+| WC-5 | **Outcome mapping.** The worker sends `worker_done` exactly once from its own top-level session. `--outcome failed` when the worker contract says `failed` or `blocked`, or no worker contract exists; `--outcome succeeded` otherwise, including `partial` and including an audit whose `verdict` is `FAIL` (the audit itself succeeded). The verdict is carried in the `verdict` field, so the coordinator checks it without reading prose; the first sentence of the body also states the status and verdict. | Worker session | `CAPS:73` (`[G]` lines 88-90, "Never encode failure only in prose"); `CAPS:76-77` |
+| WC-6 | **Resume or safe re-run.** A retry (`worker-start --task <task_id> --retry-of <dispatch_id>`) is the same Task with the same brief (`CAPS:116`), so freshness cannot rely on the coordinator changing the brief. Two cases: (a) **Output location set by the brief** (most commands): the brief writes `<dispatch_id>` into every output path and the `--contract` path (section 2.3), and the worker session replaces it with the Dispatch ID from its own preamble before running the command. Each attempt therefore writes under its own directory and a retry never touches the first attempt's files. To resume from an earlier attempt instead of starting fresh, the coordinator creates a new Task whose brief passes the command's resume flag pointing at that attempt's location; it never does so to get around a circuit-broken Task (`CAPS:116`, "Do not route around that boundary"). (b) **Fixed output location** (cleanup-audit's `.claude-audit/`, implement's ledger, save's memory keys): the command's own resume mechanism, named in section 3, handles a retry. A re-run inside one Dispatch after an `ask` follows WC-3 (3). | Command (fresh or resumable writes); worker (Dispatch ID substitution); coordinator (brief) | `CAPS:116` (retry and circuit breaker); resume mechanisms in column (e) of `INV-A:35-55`, `INV-B:31-52`; the Dispatch ID is in every worker preamble (`CAPS:148`, `[G]` line 38) |
+| WC-7 | **Run at the top level, never inside a subagent.** The worker runs the command for real in its own top-level session: a `Skill` tool call for the command's skill, or the slash command itself, made by the top-level session. Reading the command file and carrying out its steps by hand does not count, and neither does wrapping the command in an Agent-tool subagent. Lifecycle messages (`heartbeat`, `ask`, `check`, `worker_done`) are sent only by that top-level session, never by a subagent. A command's own Agent-tool fan-out stays as it is. | Worker session | `sk/sc-pr-submit-protocol/SKILL.md:37-39` (top-level rule); `CAPS:159-161`; `INV-A:95` (nesting loses tools at Claude Code's depth limit); owner decision of 2026-11-07 that a file-read-only run does not satisfy WC-7 (`/home/coder/.claude/projects/-home-coder-workspaces-IronClaude/memory/p1-c5-wc7-decided-no.md:11-15`) |
+| WC-8 | **No Orca fan-out from inside a command.** A command never calls `worker-start`, `dispatch` or `task-create`. Fan-out that needs Orca records is split into separate Tasks by the coordinator. | Command | `CAPS:97-102` (depth 1); `CAPS:103` (whether a depth-1 worker may `task-create` is UNVERIFIED) |
+| WC-9 | **No outward action unless the brief authorizes it.** Under `--unattended`, any default that pushes, opens a PR, posts to GitHub, installs, provisions or calls a paid external run is off. It turns on only through the command's own explicit flag written in the brief. | Command | `cmd/auggie-review.md:51-54` (posts by default, no prompt); `.dev/orca-command-refactor/README.md:31`; per-command outward actions in column (f) of the inventories |
+| WC-10 | **Liveness during long waits.** The worker sends a heartbeat every 5 minutes while working. A command's own Bash wait (a poll wait, a long eval) is never a single call longer than 4 minutes (240 s, so a 300 s call must also be split); longer waits are split so the session can heartbeat between them. **Exception: Agent-tool fan-out.** WC-7 keeps a command's Agent-tool fan-out unchanged, and the top-level session cannot heartbeat while one Agent-tool call runs, so such a call may exceed 5 minutes. The worker sends a heartbeat immediately before and after each Agent-tool call. The coordinator never treats a heartbeat gap as a hang: it judges a stall only by Orca's positive proof (`exited` liveness or a final agent turn with no `worker_done`), as Orca's guide requires for every absence signal. | Command (Bash wait size); worker (heartbeat); coordinator (no hang verdict from a gap) | Cadence is from the dispatch preamble, not a guide (`CAPS:130`); pr-submit's 600 s is a cumulative per-wait budget made of repeated iterations (one foreground poll, then a sleep of at least `--poll-interval`), not one blocking call (`cmd/pr-submit.md:49`; `sk/sc-pr-submit-protocol/SKILL.md:46-64`); `sk/sc-cli-eval-protocol/SKILL.md:151-152` (eval timeouts up to 3600 s) via `INV-A:65`; absence never authorizes a stall verdict, only positive proof does (`CAPS:125-126`); how long one Agent-tool fan-out runs is UNVERIFIED |
+| WC-11 | **Parallel-safe writes.** Two workers in one worktree must never write the same file. Default output paths carry a timestamp or the slug the coordinator passes; single-owner shared files (for example memory keys overwritten whole) are not written in unattended mode. | Command | `cmd/save.md:42-45`; `CAPS:115` (`[G:placement]` lines 8-11: workers share the current worktree by default) |
+| WC-12 | **Coordinator follow-ups are pulled, not pushed.** The worker runs `check` at natural checkpoints and once before `worker_done`; if `check` returns `consumer_fenced` it stops without sending `worker_done`. Commands need no change for this. | Worker session | `CAPS:66-67` (`[G:worker-contract]` lines 36-38, 49-52) |
+
+### 2.3 What the coordinator owes each worker
+The contract only works if the brief carries what the command would otherwise ask a person. For each Task the coordinator's brief states: the exact command line including `--unattended` and every flag section 3 lists as required; a `--contract <base>/<dispatch_id>/worker-contract.yaml` path and output paths under the same `<base>/<dispatch_id>/`, with the instruction that the worker replaces `<dispatch_id>` with the Dispatch ID from its preamble (WC-4, WC-6), so the coordinator knows the contract path as soon as `worker-start` returns the Dispatch ID; which outward actions, if any, are authorized (WC-9); and the worktree placement (`CAPS:115`). After `worker_done`, the coordinator opens the report file, then releases or reuses the worker (`CAPS:107-113`).
