+## 6. Phased rollout
+Each phase is independently useful and leaves standalone behaviour unchanged (section 5). Later phases do not start until the earlier phase's checks in section 7 pass. Phases 1-3 need no user decision; Phase 4 waits for answers to D2-D4.
+
+| Phase | Scope | Commands | Why this order |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **P0. Contract and live baseline** (no command edits) | Write `src/superclaude/core/WORKER-CONTRACT.md` (section 2 rules plus a coordinator brief template listing the section 3.1 flags), add it to `KNOWN_CORE_FILES`, add an Orca section to `HOW-WE-WORK.md` and its two mirrored copies, and add the static test file `tests/skills/test_worker_contract.py` (proposed name). Then run three ready commands as real Orca workers, invoked directly, to close evidence gap 1.4 (1). | Smoke runs: `/sc:troubleshoot` (no `--fix`), `/sc:workflow --handoff none`, `/sc:adversarial --output <dir>` | Uses only the 10 ready commands, so the coordinator gets value before any command changes, and the smoke runs test the decision before anything is built on it. |
+| **P1. Flag, worker contract and commands with no gate** | (a) For the 35 commands in sections 3.1 and 3.2 other than load and pr-submit (pr-submit gets them in P4, and only if the user chooses D3 (b)): `--unattended`, `--contract`, the WC-4 worker contract and the `## Unattended mode` section. For a command whose gates P2 converts, the P1 section states only the flag and contract and says its gates are not yet converted; the coordinator dispatches such a command only with a 3.1 brief that avoids its gates until its P2 change lands. (b) Output flags and the 3.2 "Other required change" for the no-gate commands. (c) The small fixes to ready commands. (d) load's usage line. | (a) every 3.1 and 3.2 command except load and pr-submit: adversarial, brainstorm, cleanup-audit, index-repo, init-lite, reflect, release-split, tdd, troubleshoot, workflow, analyze, auggie-review, build, business-panel, cleanup, cli-eval, cli-portify, design, document, explain, implement, improve, index, post-release, recommend, research, review-translation, roadmap, save, spec-panel, task, tasklist, test, validate-roadmap, validate-tests; (b) analyze, build, business-panel, design, document, explain, index, test; (c) adversarial, cleanup-audit, index-repo, workflow; (d) load | No person gate is involved, so each change is mechanical and gives every later phase a worker contract to check. |
+| **P2. Gate conversion** | Each default-on gate resolves per its section 3 row; WC-10 wait splitting for cli-eval. P2 edits only the gate lines and the command's existing Unattended section; the flag and contract already exist from P1. | auggie-review, cleanup, improve, cli-eval, cli-portify, recommend, research, review-translation, spec-panel, task, tasklist, reflect, release-split, tdd, troubleshoot | The main blocker class (`INV-A:96`, `INV-B:292`). These changes touch no safety rule and add no outward capability; where a command has an outward default (auggie-review posting, task's `commit`/`push` and committed TFEP report), P2 only turns it off under `--unattended` (WC-9). |
+| **P3. Interface drift and shared state** | Reconcile command and skill where they disagree; make memory writes parallel-safe. | roadmap, validate-roadmap, validate-tests, save | These are defects today regardless of Orca (`INV-B:294,296`); the coordinator cannot predict their report path until they are fixed. |
+| **P4. Outward actions and safety rules** | Apply the answers to D2-D4: the implement halt path, post-release pre-authorization, pr-submit as a worker. | implement, post-release, pr-submit | Each one either changes a deliberate safety rule (`sk/sc-implement-protocol/refs/qa.md:238`) or performs outward actions (`sk/sc-pr-submit-protocol/SKILL.md:189,196`; `sk/sc-post-release-protocol/SKILL.md:340,351`), so it needs the user's decision first. |
