+### 1.3 Rejected alternative: commands call Orca internally
+In the alternative, a command would itself start Orca workers for its fan-out (for example one worker per `/sc:tdd` research agent), send its own `worker_done`, and call `orca orchestration ask` for its questions. It is rejected for these reasons:
+
+| # | Reason | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| R1 | It cannot run as a worker at the current setting. Nesting depth is 1 on this machine and "1 means a coordinator dispatches workers and those workers may not dispatch further", so a command that calls `worker-start` from inside a worker would be refused with `nested_worker_depth_exceeded`. Which call sites enforce the check was not traced; `worker-start` and `dispatch` are presumed (UNVERIFIED, `CAPS:103`). A new Run does not reset depth, and Orca's own advice is "Do the work in this terminal". The two designs exclude each other unless the user raises the depth setting. | `CAPS:97-101,103`; `orca-data.json:1` quoted at `CAPS:98` |
+| R2 | It breaks standalone use. Commands are installed to `~/.claude/` and run in other projects. Without a running Orca every orchestration call fails with `runtime_unavailable`; outside an Orca terminal the caller must pass `--from`; and a session with no live preamble must not send lifecycle messages at all. | `CLAUDE.md:74`; `CAPS:147` (`cli-error.js:49-53`); `CAPS:148-149` (`[G]` line 41; `terminal-identity.js:213-215`) |
+| R3 | A command cannot own the completion message. `worker_done` is accepted only from the dispatched pane, and the Task and Dispatch IDs exist only in the preamble given to the worker session, not in the command's arguments. | `CAPS:76`, `CAPS:150` (`[H:send]` line 40); `CAPS:148` (`[G]` line 38) |
+| R4 | The executable name differs by platform and setup (`orca-ide` on Linux outside Orca terminals, an `orca` shim inside them), so command text that calls Orca would carry platform logic. | `CAPS:145` (`/opt/Orca/resources/bin/orca-ide:16`; `linux-orca-cli-shim/orca:2`) |
+| R5 | It would create a second orchestrator below the coordinator. The inventories already mark the commands that orchestrate (`/sc:agent`, `/sc:pm`, `/sc:spawn`) coordinator-only because two controllers compete. | `INV-A:90`; `INV-B:285-286` |
+
+What the alternative would have given, and how this spec gets it instead: per-unit liveness, Task records and independent retry for heavy fan-out. Orca keeps those records only for workers the coordinator starts (`CAPS:158`). Under this decision the coordinator gets them by splitting the work into separate worker Tasks (for example several `/sc:troubleshoot` workers, then one `/sc:adversarial` worker on their reports), not by commands calling Orca.
+
+### 1.4 How strong the evidence is
+The evidence supports the decision. Three gaps are stated plainly:
+1. **No command in scope has been run end to end as an Orca worker in this run.** Every verdict in both inventories comes from reading files (`INV-B:323`). The only end-to-end proof is the PR #329 run, and its brief told workers to run `/sc:adversarial` through an Agent-tool subagent (`.dev/codegraph-migration/orchestration/README.md:31`), so it proves the nested route, not the direct route this spec requires. Whether every such spawn succeeded is UNVERIFIED (`CAPS:156`, `CAPS:187`). Phase 0 (section 6) therefore starts with live smoke runs.
+2. **The 60-second readiness evidence is narrower than it looks.** It concerns a question shown *before* Claude reached its input box (`EVIDENCE.md:39`, a shell wrapper's session-name prompt). A command's own `AskUserQuestion` or chat question comes *after* readiness, so it does not fail `worker-start`; Orca reports such a worker as waiting, "healthy, not failed" (`CAPS:52`, `CAPS:129`). The real failure mode for a command prompt is a silent stall with no `worker_done`, which the coordinator sees only through `worker-show`'s `agentWait` or a missing report. Section 2 covers both cases.
+3. **Agent-tool fan-out inside a worker has no proven limit, but no proof of none either.** No Orca limit on subagents was found, and whether a subagent's Bash `send` would be accepted as the worker's own is UNVERIFIED (`CAPS:155`, `CAPS:159-160`, `CAPS:186`). The contract forbids subagents from sending lifecycle messages, so the design does not depend on that answer.
+
+## 2. The worker contract
+
+### 2.1 Terms
+- **Worker run**: a session that received an Orca preamble carrying a Task ID and a Dispatch ID. Only such a session may send lifecycle messages; a session the user started, inside or outside Orca, is an ordinary session (`CAPS:148`, `[G]` lines 38 and 41).
+- **Unattended mode**: the behaviour a command switches to when its caller passes `--unattended`. It is the only command-side change the contract asks for besides report files and resume. It names no Orca command, so the same mode serves any caller that has no person at the keyboard. How the mode is selected (flag versus detecting the preamble) is open decision D1 in section 9; this spec is written for the recommended flag.
+- **Contract file**: one shared document, proposed as `src/superclaude/core/WORKER-CONTRACT.md`, installed to `~/.claude/WORKER-CONTRACT.md`. Install copies every `*.md` in `src/superclaude/core/` to `~/.claude/` (`src/superclaude/cli/install_core.py:4,33,53-70`; `CLAUDE.md:83,408`), so the new file installs with no code change. `KNOWN_CORE_FILES` (`src/superclaude/cli/install_core.py:12-24`) only filters the installed-files listing (`install_core.py:152`); the new name is added there so the listing reports it. Each changed command adds a short `## Unattended mode` section whose first line is "Read `~/.claude/WORKER-CONTRACT.md` before any other step", because an installed core file that `CLAUDE.md` does not import is read only when something tells the agent to read it (the routing guide is documented as "not loaded automatically", `src/superclaude/core/CLAUDE.md:46,75`); the section then lists only the command's own gate resolutions and report file, so the shared rules are written once and still applied. The contract file holds four parts: the command-side rules (WC-2, WC-4, WC-6, WC-8, WC-9, WC-10 wait size, WC-11), the status and verdict adapter (section 2.4), the **worker-session block** (section 2.3) and the coordinator brief template that embeds that block. The worker-session duties cannot rely on the command's Unattended section, because that section is read only after the command was invoked; they reach the worker through the brief instead (section 2.3).
+- **Installed copy under test**: a worker loads commands, skills and the contract file from `~/.claude/` (this worktree has no project `.claude/commands/sc` or `.claude/skills`; whether a project copy would win over the user copy is UNVERIFIED). `make sync-dev` copies skills, agents, commands, hooks and templates into the project `.claude/`, not core files (`Makefile:109-165`), so `~/.claude/WORKER-CONTRACT.md` exists only after `superclaude install` (`src/superclaude/cli/install_core.py:33,53-70`), which replaces the framework in `~/.claude/` for every Claude session on the machine (and also installs the pinned codegraph binary, `src/superclaude/cli/main.py:46-52`). Live checks therefore run only against installed copies that match the branch byte for byte (section 7.2, step P), installed under open decision D6.
+- **Worker contract**: the WC-4 file `worker-contract.yaml`, written at the path the caller passes with `--contract <path>`. It is a separate file from any `return-contract.yaml` a command already writes, so existing contracts keep their own schema and status values unchanged.
+
+### 2.2 Requirements
+Each requirement says what the command must do, what the worker session (the top-level agent that received the preamble) must do, and the evidence.
+
+| ID | Requirement | Owner | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| WC-1 | **No prompt before the agent is ready.** Nothing in the launch path (wrapper, hook, startup command) may wait on a person before Claude's input box is ready. Any such prompt must fall back on its own well inside the readiness limit (default 60 s). | Launch setup, not commands | `EVIDENCE.md:39` (worker-start failed at `agent_readiness` behind a session-name prompt); `EVIDENCE.md:45,56` (passes with a 30 s fallback; above about 45 s it fails again); `CAPS:44-45` (60 s default, `--timeout-ms` per start) |
+| WC-2 | **No person-blocking step in unattended mode.** Under `--unattended` the command never calls `AskUserQuestion`, never ends its turn on a chat question or `[yes / no]` offer, and never says STOP AND WAIT. Each gate resolves one of three ways, listed per command in section 3: (a) a documented default; (b) a value the caller supplied as a flag; (c) **blocked**: the command writes its report with `status: blocked` and the exact question, and stops. | Command | Gates per command at `INV-A:76-83`, `INV-B:292`; a worker waiting on a prompt is "healthy", so a stall is invisible (`CAPS:52`, `CAPS:129`); a plain chat offer also stalls (`INV-A:62`) |
+| WC-3 | **Questions go to the coordinator, not to a local prompt.** When a command ends `blocked` and the worker session holds a preamble, the session may ask the coordinator with the preamble's `ask` command, wait, then re-invoke the command with the answer. Rules: (1) A gate is answerable through `ask` only when its section 3 row names the flag or argument that carries the answer; any other gate (for example cli-portify's approval gates, implement's Ruling) is not answerable this way, and the worker reports the blocked contract. (2) At most one `ask` per gate per Dispatch; a second gate hit after the re-run gets its own single `ask`. (3) The re-run uses the same command line plus the answering flag, the same `--contract` path and the same output location; it overwrites the blocked worker contract, which is the only file a re-run is meant to replace (WC-6 freshness applies per Dispatch, not per re-run). (4) An empty reply, or a reply that names no usable value, counts as no answer: the worker keeps the blocked contract and reports it. (5) On an `ask` timeout the session resumes the same message ID and never creates a duplicate, at most 3 times (4 waits in all); after that it reports the blocked contract. No heartbeat is sent while blocked in `ask`. | Worker session | `CAPS:58-61` (`[H:ask]` lines 3-5, 23-24; `[G]` lines 80-82); `CAPS:64` (`[G:worker-contract]` lines 10-11) |
+| WC-4 | **One worker contract, at a path known before the command runs, written on every outcome.** `--unattended` requires `--contract <path>`; the command refuses `--unattended` without it (a chat-only refusal, since there is no path to write). The command writes `worker-contract.yaml` at that path on success, failure, blocked and partial outcomes, including stops before any other output exists (for workflow, pre-package STOPs such as `E-LEGACY`, which today write no yaml by design). One flag serves every output class: directory outputs (`--output <dir>`), file outputs (tdd and init-lite take a file, `cmd/tdd.md:49`, `cmd/init-lite.md:32`), generated packages (workflow's `<id>` is generated and `--output` is retired) and nested paths (adversarial's own contract). The file is separate from any `return-contract.yaml` a command already writes; those keep their schema and values unchanged (SA-1). Fields, at least: `contract_version` (`"1.0"`), `status` (one of `succeeded`, `partial`, `failed`, `blocked`), `verdict` (`PASS` or `FAIL` for exactly the commands section 2.4 marks `PASS/FAIL`, derived by that row's rule; `n/a` for every other command), `own_status` (the command's own status or verdict string verbatim, or null when it has none), `command` (the exact command line), `report_path` (the main human-readable deliverable, or null), `artifacts` (every other file written, including the command's own `return-contract.yaml` if any), `blocked_question` (when blocked), and `next_command` (when `blocked` or `failed`: the exact command a person or the coordinator runs to finish or resume, or null when none exists). Translation from a command's own status follows the adapter in section 2.4, which is total: every own value a command documents today has a row, and any value the adapter does not list becomes `failed` with the value kept in `own_status`, so a new or unexpected value is never reported as a success. The worker passes the `--contract` path as `worker_done --report-path`. Orca does not check the file, so the coordinator opens it. | Command writes; worker reports; coordinator reads | `CAPS:72-74` (`--report-path` only with real values); `CAPS:79` (Orca's handling of the file is UNVERIFIED); existing status and verdict values: section 2.4 Evidence column |
+| WC-5 | **Outcome mapping.** The worker sends `worker_done` exactly once from its own top-level session. `--outcome failed` when the worker contract says `failed` or `blocked`, or no worker contract exists; `--outcome succeeded` otherwise, including `partial` and including an audit whose `verdict` is `FAIL` (the audit itself succeeded). The verdict is carried in the `verdict` field, so the coordinator checks it without reading prose; the first sentence of the body also states the status and verdict. | Worker session | `CAPS:73` (`[G]` lines 88-90, "Never encode failure only in prose"); `CAPS:76-77` |
+| WC-6 | **Resume or safe re-run.** A retry (`worker-start --task <task_id> --retry-of <dispatch_id>`) is the same Task with the same brief (`CAPS:116`), so freshness cannot rely on the coordinator changing the brief. Two cases: (a) **Output location set by the brief** (most commands): the brief writes `<dispatch_id>` into every output path and the `--contract` path (section 2.3), and the worker session replaces it with the Dispatch ID from its own preamble before running the command. Each attempt therefore writes under its own directory and a retry never touches the first attempt's files. To resume from an earlier attempt instead of starting fresh, the coordinator creates a new Task whose brief passes the command's resume flag pointing at that attempt's location; it never does so to get around a circuit-broken Task (`CAPS:116`, "Do not route around that boundary"). (b) **Fixed output location** (cleanup-audit's `.claude-audit/`, implement's ledger, save's memory keys): the command's own resume mechanism, named in section 3, handles a retry. A re-run inside one Dispatch after an `ask` follows WC-3 (3). | Command (fresh or resumable writes); worker (Dispatch ID substitution); coordinator (brief) | `CAPS:116` (retry and circuit breaker); resume mechanisms in column (e) of `INV-A:35-55`, `INV-B:31-52`; the Dispatch ID is in every worker preamble (`CAPS:148`, `[G]` line 38) |
+| WC-7 | **Run at the top level, never inside a subagent.** The worker runs the command for real in its own top-level session: a `Skill` tool call whose skill name is the command's own name `sc:<name>` (the name the brief gives), or the slash command `/sc:<name>` itself, made by the top-level session. A call to the command's protocol skill (for example `sc:workflow-protocol`) made by the top-level session also counts, because a command whose file names a skill must make that call before any other output (`CLAUDE.md:11`; for example `cmd/workflow.md:51`); a call made only from inside a subagent does not. The brief's worker-session block (section 2.3) is what tells the worker to do this. Reading the command file and carrying out its steps by hand does not count, and neither does wrapping the command in an Agent-tool subagent. Lifecycle messages (`heartbeat`, `ask`, `check`, `worker_done`) are sent only by that top-level session, never by a subagent. A command's own Agent-tool fan-out stays as it is. | Worker session | `sk/sc-pr-submit-protocol/SKILL.md:37-39` (top-level rule); `CAPS:159-161`; `INV-A:95` (nesting loses tools at Claude Code's depth limit); owner decision of 2026-11-07 that a file-read-only run does not satisfy WC-7 (`/home/coder/.claude/projects/-home-coder-workspaces-IronClaude/memory/p1-c5-wc7-decided-no.md:11-15`) |
