+## 8. Non-goals
+1. **Commands calling Orca.** No command, skill or agent gains an Orca call, a `worker_done`, or Orca-specific branching (section 1.3, SA-2).
+2. **Changing Orca or its settings.** Nesting depth stays 1 unless the user decides otherwise (D5); no change to Orca's readiness limit, guides or code (`CAPS:98`).
+3. **Building a coordinator program.** The coordinator is an Orca coordinator session following Orca's own guide (`CAPS:12-13`); this spec adds a brief template, not a new CLI, daemon or scheduler.
+4. **Turning coordinator-only commands into workers** (section 4).
+5. **Removing or weakening any interactive behaviour for people** (section 5). `--unattended` is opt-in.
+6. **Changing the `/sc:implement` Ruling rule** unless the user chooses D2 option (b) (`sk/sc-implement-protocol/refs/qa.md:238`).
+7. **Making Agent-tool subagents into Orca workers**, or letting subagents send lifecycle messages (WC-7).
+8. **Fixing every defect the inventories list.** Only defects that stop a command working as a worker are in scope. Others stay recorded in the inventories, for example `/sc:agent`'s missing opening frontmatter line (`INV-A:36`) and the cleanup/improve rollback claims (`INV-A:118`).
+9. **Remote workers, model and effort choice, and cost tuning.** `--on <environment>`, `--model` and `--effort` are coordinator choices Orca already supports (`CAPS:128`, `CAPS:135-139`); this spec does not set them.
+10. **Implementing anything in this run.** This is a planning spec (`.dev/orca-command-refactor/README.md:6,30`).
+
+## 9. Open decisions for the user
+Six decisions belong to the user. Each is self-contained, with a recommendation. Phases 0-3 can proceed on the recommendations, except that their live checks wait for D6; Phase 4 waits for D2-D4.
+
+**D1. How does a command know it is running without a person?**
+- (a) **Explicit `--unattended` flag in the brief (recommended).** The coordinator writes it into every worker's command line; a person never types it. Example: `/sc:troubleshoot "login fails" --unattended --contract .dev/troubleshoot/login/<dispatch_id>/worker-contract.yaml`. It is testable by grep and cannot misfire on a person.
+- (b) **Detect Orca.** The command checks `ORCA_TERMINAL_HANDLE` or looks for a worker preamble in the conversation. The variable is set in Orca terminals (`CAPS:149`, observed in a dispatched worker at `CAPS:151`); whether it is also set in a person's own Orca tab is UNVERIFIED, and if it is, prompts would vanish for a person working in Orca, breaking SA-1; preamble detection depends on the model reading prose and cannot be tested mechanically.
+- Recommendation: (a).
+
+**D2. When `/sc:implement` halts for a Ruling inside a worker, who may give it?**
+- Today a Ruling is accepted from "clear user confirmation in the current conversation" or from a valid existing operator Ruling, and "another agent's message" does not count (`sk/sc-implement-protocol/refs/qa.md:232,238`). An Orca coordinator is another agent.
+- (a) **Keep the rule (recommended).** The worker stops, writes the halt to its ledger and contract, and ends `failed`. The person reads the halt and gives the Ruling by running `/sc:implement <plan>` in their own session, which resumes from the ledger.
+- (b) **Allow a relayed Ruling.** The coordinator asks the person, then answers the worker's `ask` with the person's exact words, which the worker records verbatim as the Ruling. Faster, but it weakens a deliberate safety rule and trusts the coordinator to relay faithfully.
+- Recommendation: (a), because the rule exists to stop an agent approving its own work.
+
+**D3. Should `/sc:pr-submit` run as an Orca worker at all?**
+- It opens the PR, commits fixes, pushes, and replies to and resolves review threads, all outward actions (`sk/sc-pr-submit-protocol/SKILL.md:189,196`), and runs an attended poll loop whose waits have a 600-second default budget over several rounds (`cmd/pr-submit.md:49`; `sk/sc-pr-submit-protocol/SKILL.md:41-64`). The repo requires it for every PR (`CLAUDE.md:50`).
+- (a) **Coordinator or person only (recommended).** The coordinator (a top-level session) runs it after the workers finish; no pr-submit changes in Phase 4.
+- (b) **Worker, with pre-authorization.** Apply the section 3 row (wait splitting, summary file, HALTs as blocked) so several PRs can be monitored in parallel.
+- Recommendation: (a), unless parallel PR monitoring is a real need.
+
+**D4. May a coordinator's brief authorize a worker's outward actions?** (affects post-release, pr-submit under D3 (b), auggie-review `--post-pr`, task `commit`/`push`)
+- (a) **Only when the user's own instruction to the coordinator names that action for that task (recommended).** The coordinator quotes the instruction in the brief and passes the command's explicit flag; without it every outward default is off (WC-9).
+- (b) **Never.** Workers make no outward changes; the coordinator or person performs every push, PR, comment, install and VM step after reading the reports.
+- Recommendation: (a). It matches the repo's own rule that post-release confirms outward steps "unless the user pre-authorized them this session" (`sk/sc-post-release-protocol/SKILL.md:340,351`).
+
+**D5. Should Orca's nesting depth be raised so commands could start their own Orca workers?**
+- Today depth is 1: workers cannot start workers (`CAPS:97-98`). Raising it is a setting in Orca's desktop app (`CAPS:99`).
+- (a) **Keep depth 1 (recommended).** This spec does not need deeper nesting; fan-out stays inside commands or is split into Tasks by the coordinator.
+- (b) **Raise it.** Would allow the rejected alternative (section 1.3) and break R1's protection against runaway fan-out.
+- Recommendation: (a).
+
+**D6. How do live checks get the branch's commands, skills and contract file where a worker loads them?**
+- A worker loads `~/.claude/` (section 2.1, "Installed copy under test"). `make sync-dev` does not copy core files, so the only route that also installs `~/.claude/WORKER-CONTRACT.md` is `superclaude install`, which replaces the framework for every Claude session on the machine, including the coordinator's own, until it is reinstalled from master.
+- (a) **Install the branch into `~/.claude/` for each phase's live checks, with the user's go-ahead each time (recommended).** Before a phase's first live check the coordinator asks the user, then runs `uv run superclaude install --force` from the branch checkout; 7.2 step P confirms the copies match; after the phase the user decides whether to reinstall from master. Example: before AC-1.2, the user approves "install branch `<branch>` into `~/.claude/` now".
+- (b) **Run live checks only on a separate machine or user account** whose `~/.claude/` serves no other work. Nothing else on the main machine changes, but that environment must have Orca, Claude Code and this repository set up first.
+- Recommendation: (a), because it needs no new environment and the per-phase approval keeps the machine-wide change visible.
+
+## Decisions
+Simple choices made by this worker.
+
+| Decision | Options considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Name of the unattended switch | `--unattended`, `--worker`, `--orca`, `--non-interactive` | `--unattended` names the condition (no person), not a tool, so it stays meaningful for any caller (SA-2). `--non-interactive` reads as the opposite of the existing opt-in `--interactive` flags, which are already off by default (`INV-B:301`), so it would not signal the stronger rule (no gates, no outward defaults). |
+| Report shape (revised in Task 6) | A per-command format; reuse `return-contract.yaml`; a separate `worker-contract.yaml` | Separate file. The original choice (reuse `return-contract.yaml`) collided with the existing contracts' status values and locations (review findings 1 and 2: `sk/sc-workflow-protocol/refs/return-contract.md:7,39`, `sk/sc-brainstorm-protocol/SKILL.md:341`, `sk/sc-troubleshoot-protocol/SKILL.md:65`). A separate file keeps every existing contract unchanged (SA-1) and still gives the coordinator one schema to check. |
+| Contract location (Task 6) | Derive it per output class (directory, file, package); one `--contract <path>` flag | One flag: it covers directory outputs, file outputs (tdd, init-lite), workflow's generated package and pre-package STOPs with one rule, and checks no `--contract` flag exists today (`grep -rnE -- '--contract\b' src/superclaude/commands src/superclaude/skills` printed nothing on 2026-11-08). |
+| Status vocabulary | Copy each command's own statuses; a fixed four-value set | Fixed set `succeeded / partial / failed / blocked` in the worker contract, mapped to Orca's two outcomes in WC-5, because Orca accepts only `succeeded` or `failed` (`CAPS:73`). Commands' own statuses are translated per WC-4. |
+| brainstorm `dry-run` (Task 6) | `succeeded`; `partial`; refuse `--dry-run` under `--unattended` | `succeeded`, only when the brief passed `--dry-run`: the run did what the coordinator asked. Refusing would remove a coordinator option for no stated reason. |
+| troubleshoot `blocked` (Task 6) | `partial` (report exists); `blocked` | `blocked`, with the external action as `blocked_question`: the skill's own meaning is "cannot proceed without an external action" (`sk/sc-troubleshoot-protocol/SKILL.md:65`), which the coordinator must see as an action item, not a usable finish. The report is still listed in `report_path`. |
+| `ask` timeout bound (Task 6) | Unbounded resume; a fixed bound | At most 3 resumes (4 waits); at Orca's guide value of 600000 ms per wait (`CAPS:60`) that is about 40 minutes before the worker reports the blocked contract. |
+| Heartbeat during Agent-tool fan-out (Task 6) | Exempt Agent-tool calls from WC-10; require background fan-out | Exempt: requiring background agents would change every fan-out command's structure, which WC-7 keeps unchanged; Orca's guide already forbids a stall verdict from absence alone (`CAPS:125-126`). |
+| pr-submit in P1 (Task 6) | Add `--unattended` in P1 with the others; only in P4 under D3 (b) | P4 only: D3 recommends coordinator-only, and P1 is meant to need no user decision (section 6 intro). |
+| AC-C1 scope (Task 6) | Keep `core/` in scope with an exclusion list; drop `core/` | Drop `core/`: SA-2 constrains commands, skills and agents; `core/` gains Orca text by design in P0. |
+| `partial` maps to `succeeded` | `succeeded`; `failed` | A partial run left a usable deliverable; `failed` would tell the coordinator the work is lost. The body's first sentence and the contract still say `partial` (WC-5), so nothing is hidden. A worker's own `failed` report does not trip Orca's circuit breaker either way (`CAPS:80`). |
+| Where shared rules live | Repeat them in every command; one core file referenced by installed path | One core file (`~/.claude/WORKER-CONTRACT.md`), following the installed-path rule (`CLAUDE.md:72-83`) and the write-once rule. |
+| `/sc:load` handling | Add a report file; use it only inside another worker's brief | Use it inside a brief: its only output is context for the current session (`INV-B:65`); a report file would be an unrequested addition. Only its usage-line defect is fixed. |
+| Wait size for WC-10 | 5 minutes; 4 minutes | 4 minutes leaves a margin under the 5-minute heartbeat cadence (`CAPS:130`). |
+| Phase order | By inventory group; by risk | By risk: no-gate commands first, then gates, then drift, then safety rules and outward actions, so every later phase has report files to check and user decisions are needed only at the end. |
+| Inventory verdicts | Re-judge commands; keep the inventories' 10 / 27 / 6 split | Keep: both inventories were independently citation-checked (`INV-A:106`, `INV-B:320`) and nothing read in this task contradicted a verdict. `/sc:load` and `/sc:recommend` are noted as coordinator-side helpers without changing their verdict. |
+| Evidence-strength statement | Present the decision as proven; state the gaps | State the gaps (section 1.4): no direct-route end-to-end run exists yet, so Phase 0 tests the decision first. |
+| Status adapter (Task 8) | Per-command translation lines in WC-4; one adapter table plus a default rule | One table (section 2.4) with a default that maps any unlisted own value to `failed`: the translation is total today and stays safe when a command adds a value. `own_status` keeps the command's own word, so nothing is lost by the mapping. |
+| release-split decisions (Task 8) | `no-split` → `partial`; all three decisions → `succeeded` | `succeeded`: each decision is a finished analysis the coordinator asked for; which decision was made is in `own_status`, not a quality signal. |
+| Conditional verdicts (Task 8) | Both conditional values → `PASS`; both → `FAIL`; each by its own definition | By definition: review-translation's `CONDITIONAL PASS` is a pass with limits (score ≥ 70, no Critical, at most 3 High, `sk/sc-review-translation-protocol/SKILL.md:99`) → `PASS`; validate-roadmap's `CONDITIONAL_GO` requires corrections before proceeding (`sk/sc-validate-roadmap-protocol/SKILL.md:684,965`) → `FAIL`. The exact word is in `own_status` either way. |
+| reflect `needs_human_decision` (Task 8) | `blocked`; `succeeded` with `verdict: FAIL` | `succeeded` with `FAIL`: the audit finished and produced its report; no flag or `ask` reply could complete it, which is what `blocked` means in WC-2 (c). |
+| Which commands carry a verdict (Task 8) | Every audit-like command; only commands with a documented pass/fail result | Only the 7 with a documented pass/fail rule (section 2.4). cleanup-audit, analyze and adversarial produce findings or merges, not a pass/fail result, so they write `n/a`. |
+| Carrier for worker-session duties (Task 8) | In the command's Unattended section; in the contract file only; in the brief | The brief (section 2.3): it is the only text the worker holds before it invokes the command, which is when WC-7 applies. The block's wording is written once, in the contract file's brief template, and copied verbatim. |
+| AC-C1 pattern (Task 8) | List more subcommands; match any `orca` word | Any word starting with `orca`: SA-2 forbids every Orca mention outside core, so a subcommand list can only under-detect; the broad form still prints nothing today. |
+| AC-C5 comparison (Task 8) | Exempt Usage and `argument-hint:` lines; compare word by word | Word by word: an exemption would let any edit to those lines through, while a word diff allows only insertions and needs no exception list. |
+| Snapshot for 7.2 step 6 (Task 8) | `git status --short --untracked-files=all --ignored` plus per-line hashes; a start timestamp and `find -newer` | Timestamp plus `find`: it lists each written file, including re-modified and gitignored files, without hashing every ignored file; the porcelain diff is kept for deletions. |
+| Coordinator reply in gate-trigger runs (Task 8) | Do not reply; reply with a fixed no-value text | Fixed text `NO ANSWER: gate-trigger test`: WC-3 (4) already defines a reply with no usable value as no answer, so the worker settles at once instead of waiting about 40 minutes. |
